========================================================================================
 Denied request trace: chronological denial log with candidate policies, in LogScale
========================================================================================
 The question this answers: a token got "permission denied." Which of its policies did
 it, and what else was this identity doing around the same time?

 Confirm syntax and field names against one real denied-request audit entry in your own
 environment before relying on any result. The field logic below reflects how Vault's
 audit log is structured; this file's LogScale query syntax has not been run against a
 live LogScale instance and may need a small adjustment.

 WHAT THIS CANNOT DO, BY DESIGN OF THE AUDIT LOG ITSELF (not a query limitation):
 - On a DENIAL, auth.policy_results is just {"allowed": false}. Vault does not log which
   policies it evaluated or which one failed. There is no "the one that denied it" field
   anywhere in the event.
 - On an ALLOWED request, auth.policy_results.granting_policies names the exact policy
   that granted it, including its namespace_path. This is real attribution, but only for
   the requests that succeeded, which is not the case you're usually investigating.
 - So for a DENIAL, the best this query can do is a CANDIDATE set: auth.policies UNION
   auth.external_namespace_policies[request.namespace.id]. The one lacking a matching
   capability on the denied path is the likely culprit, checked by hand (see the
   companion scenario doc, Step 2) -- a candidate set, not an attribution.
 - It cannot tell you whether a Sentinel RGP/EGP fired instead of an ACL check, or what
   that looks like in `error`. If the error string differs by denial type (ACL vs RGP
   vs EGP), capture a real example and update this file and the companion scenario doc.
 - auth.policies IS the full merged candidate set for SAME-namespace grants, group-derived
   policies included, not limited to auth.token_policies. A group-granted policy shows up
   in auth.policies AND separately in auth.identity_policies on every request, success or
   denial.
 - A CROSS-namespace grant (an external group in one namespace granting a policy via
   membership in an internal group inside a different namespace) does NOT show up in
   auth.policies or auth.identity_policies at all. It only appears in
   auth.external_namespace_policies, a map KEYED BY NAMESPACE ID (not path):
   {"<namespace_id>": ["<policy>", ...]}. A naive query on auth.policies alone will show
   a cross-namespace-granted token as default-only, which is a false negative, not an
   empty result. Always union both fields; see query 1 below.
 - The external_namespace_policies map is keyed by namespace ID, not path. Join
   request.namespace.id (present on every request) against the map's keys, never
   request.namespace.path. namespace_path only appears inside policy_results on ALLOWED
   events; build an ID-to-path lookup from those if you need one.
 - If request.namespace.id is NOT a key in auth.external_namespace_policies, that itself
   is the finding: the identity has no cross-namespace grant in the namespace where the
   denial happened, even if it has one elsewhere.
 - Confirm on your own cluster: how auth.external_namespace_policies renders under your
   audit device's HMAC settings, and whether the same-namespace (identity_policies) and
   cross-namespace (external_namespace_policies) fields both populate correctly when a
   single token carries both kinds of grant at once.
 - request.remote_address may not be the real client IP if your load balancer does not
   preserve it (see grafana/README.md). Don't build attribution on it without confirming.

 Nested JSON fields come through the audit event body; adjust the parse stage below to
 however your LogScale repo is configured (#type/structuredData vs raw JSON parse).
========================================================================================


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 1. Chronological denial trace for one path or one identity
     The "search through X Y Z path and get the trace, chronological" view: walk every
     denial against one path, or everything one identity hit, in time order. Swap the
     filter line for either a path or an identity; leave the other broad to see
     everything nearby.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#repo=vault_audit
| error = *
// pick ONE of the next two lines, comment out the other
| request.path = "secret/data/prod/db-creds"
// | auth.display_name = "oidc-jsmith"
| sort(@timestamp, order=asc)
| table([@timestamp, request.namespace.id, request.operation, request.path,
         auth.display_name, auth.entity_id, auth.policies, auth.identity_policies,
         auth.external_namespace_policies, auth.policy_results, error])


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 2. Candidate-policy summary: which policies show up on denials, by path
     Rolls the trace above into one row per (namespace, path, identity): every policy
     that was on the token at denial time (same-namespace and cross-namespace), and how
     many denials. Still a candidate set, not an attribution -- see status note above.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#repo=vault_audit
| error = *
| groupBy([request.namespace.id, request.path, auth.display_name, auth.entity_id],
          function=[count(as=denials),
                    collect(auth.policies, as=candidate_policies),
                    collect(auth.external_namespace_policies, as=cross_ns_candidates),
                    collect(error, as=error_samples),
                    max(@timestamp, as=last_denied)])
| sort(denials, order=desc)


----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
 3. Sanity check: does this repo have denial events at all, and do they carry auth.policies?
     RUN THIS FIRST against a real environment. If candidate_policy_coverage is 0, the
     audit device or the LogScale parser is dropping the auth block and nothing below
     this line will ever return a usable candidate list.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#repo=vault_audit
| error = *
| test(auth.policies != null) | count(as=with_policies)
